00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:57.260
for this audience.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.090
I'm awfully excited about what
I get to do in a little bit.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.200
Do they have the video,
'cause I'd love to play it.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.680
- [Jan] Yes, we have the video.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.613
Could you roll the Finland video?

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:16.700
- Oh, this is gonna be good.
- All right.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.047
- [Gary] How much would it
cost you to shave your head?

00:01:18.047 --> 00:01:20.000
No no, shave the head.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.230
Shave your head, I mean,
that's an interesting one.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.440
You're fine.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.273
What about you?

00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:28.380
- I'm cheap.
- How much?

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.170
- So how much?
- A hundred dollars.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.090
- [Gary] This is Jan and I in
Sweden and I was asking people

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:37.240
how much it would cost to shave their head

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.914
and Jan said all he needed was $100.

00:01:40.914 --> 00:01:42.066
Where's Baben?

00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:43.164
Get on it.

00:01:43.164 --> 00:01:44.327
We're doing this, it's a 100%
blog, we're shaving his head

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.941
for a hundred dollars.

00:01:46.941 --> 00:01:48.614
(Baben speaks indistinctly)

00:01:48.614 --> 00:01:49.447
Oh, even better.

00:01:49.447 --> 00:01:51.930
So, this is Baben, one of my video guys

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.493
and he's now running around trying to find

00:01:55.493 --> 00:01:57.240
(chuckles)
a razor.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.073
- [Baben] How's it going?

00:01:58.073 --> 00:01:59.020
Do you guys have razors, like,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.320
if, in theory, I needed
to cut my hair off?

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.420
- [Jan] In theory.
(chuckles)

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.320
- [Gary] In theory, to cut people's...

00:02:06.902 --> 00:02:07.735
- [Jan] But we all--

00:02:07.735 --> 00:02:10.643
- Blog, I'm very upset, Baben has failed.

00:02:11.757 --> 00:02:14.143
Leb, one, Baben, zero.

00:02:15.005 --> 00:02:17.330
We are very fortunate or unfortunate--

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.526
- Unfortunate, I want to do
it for Finland, you know.

00:02:19.526 --> 00:02:21.733
One hundred, one hundred, boom.

00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:24.160
- We should shave a hundred in his head.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.800
How much would it cost for
you to shave your head?

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.849
Alright.
- Alright, thank you.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:29.850
- Jan is--
- So...

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.300
- What I respect about Jan is
that he's a man of his word.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.690
We made a bet to shave his
head for a hundred bucks.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.670
He said, when you get to
Helsinki, we will take care of it.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.725
So, I'm going to pull
out my hundred dollars.

00:02:43.725 --> 00:02:46.848
(audience cheers)

00:02:46.848 --> 00:02:48.654
- [Jan] Alright, so I
remove my microphone.

00:02:48.654 --> 00:02:49.545
- [Gary] Here you go, my friend.

00:02:49.545 --> 00:02:51.360
- Well, it's done yet, is it?
- Well, go ahead, here you go.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.022
- That's for you.
- Alright, thank you.

00:02:53.022 --> 00:02:54.777
- Go ahead, put that on.
- Thank you.

00:02:54.777 --> 00:02:57.682
- It's a real hundred dollar bill.

00:02:57.682 --> 00:02:58.515
- [Man] We didn't even get euros?

00:02:58.515 --> 00:02:59.493
- Not even a euro.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.700
- [Jan] Well, I'm going to
excuse myself and you can--

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.110
- I've never shaved a
man's head before, so...

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.281
I hope this doesn't hurt you.

00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:12.170
(chuckles)

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.580
Good?
- Don't worry--

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.803
- Alright, I'm going to
do the first cut, then,

00:03:15.803 --> 00:03:17.480
I'm going to move you on
over, give the keynote

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:18.967
'cause that's what you're here for, but.

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:20.450
Are you ready?
- Yes.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.774
- Let's clap it up for this gentleman.

00:03:22.774 --> 00:03:26.740
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:27.593
Hello, D-Rock.

00:03:28.444 --> 00:03:30.519
(clippers buzz)

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:31.602
- [Man] Yeah!

00:03:38.073 --> 00:03:38.990
Yeah, whoo!

00:03:39.903 --> 00:03:41.303
- This is actually fun.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.970
Just do a little more.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:48.043
- Don't post it anywhere.
(audience laughs)

00:03:50.085 --> 00:03:52.990
(audience murmurs)

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.198
- This is fucking awesome.

00:03:55.198 --> 00:03:58.031
(audience laughs)

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.968
Alright, that's good,
let's clap it up for him.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:04.780
(audience applauds)

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.958
You're a real sport, you look great.

00:04:06.958 --> 00:04:08.210
- Yeah?
- Great.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.576
- Good morning.

00:04:09.576 --> 00:04:10.909
- [Man] Morning.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.870
- So, the format that we've
set up here is I'm going to

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:19.140
give a 20 to 30 minute, kind
of, 20 or so minute keynote

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.400
and then, I'm going to open
up for about 20, 25 minutes

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.910
of Q and A and so, the
thing that I really wanted

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:29.120
to talk about this morning was
a couple of different pillars

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.990
that I'm seeing in the
marketplace that I think will help

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.470
the individuals in this room
and I think the word that

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.130
I want to focus on most of all
this morning is practicality.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.620
I think, when I think about
the makeup of the individuals

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.460
in this room, whether they come
from a corporate environment

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.630
or in a startup an entrepreneur,

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.820
two years into their business,
20 years into their business,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.610
I think the thing that
really resonates with me is,

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.270
especially at this point in
the evolution of the internet,

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:58.440
which is really what we're
all kind of sitting here

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.533
and talking about,

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.670
there's a really interesting
delta of the people

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:08.200
in this audience, in this
market of Helsinki, in Europe,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.050
the U.S., mainland China,
the people that are clearly

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.000
accelerating are the ones that understand

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.150
the practicality of the moment.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:18.940
Not where Blockchain is going,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.860
not what television commercials
used to do for them,

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.860
but who's actually marketing
and executing in a May/June

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:33.440
2018 world and taking
advantage of where all of our

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.040
collective attention is
and what to do with it

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:39.780
based on their business objectives today

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:40.860
and within the short term.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.240
And so, for me, the thing
that's most interesting is that

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.950
in a world where things like Ai and AR

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:52.530
and the emerging Blockchains
that I think are going to be

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:56.650
built in the next couple of
years and in a world of voice,

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.140
which I'm unbelievably
passionate about and really,

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.930
I do think that the apps
built on top of Alexa

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.670
and Google Home, Apple Pod
are, no question, going to have

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:09.060
the same impact on our
society the way that Waze,

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:13.210
or Spotify, or Shopify,
or Instagram has as apps

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.220
on top of the iPhone.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.734
In that whole world, to me,
that feels like 12, 24, 36,

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:24.290
48, 60 months from where
it starts making an impact.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:29.290
To me, if I have any impact
on this audience today,

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:32.670
the places where I want
to go on or where I think

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.730
you can have the biggest return
on what you're trying to do.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.780
Regardless of what you're
doing, whether you're developing

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.650
an incredibly large project
that you're working on,

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:45.730
whether you're selling
sneakers, whether it's T-shirts,

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.870
or your services, whether
you're a SaaS company,

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.350
a media company, no matter
what anybody does here,

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:53.640
running for mayor, no matter
what anybody does here,

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.880
the currency is attention.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.340
The currency that we all
strive for is attention.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.580
And then, once you have the
attention, the game that we all

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.580
need to play is the ability to
create words in written form,

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:12.350
audio, video, or pictures
to inspire an action

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.140
that we're intrigued by.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.120
For me, that plays out very simply.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.720
When I look at the landscape today,

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.330
this is the remote
control and the platform

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:23.390
of our attention.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:26.260
And when you look at the data
of how much time is spent

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.440
on YouTube, and Facebook,
and Twitter, and Instagram,

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.800
and LinkedIn on this device,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.020
social networks or content
platforms that we now call

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.170
social networks represent
almost half the time

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.650
humans spend on a cell phone.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.090
I think we can all agree
that the amount of time

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:45.660
that we spend on a cell
phone is staggering

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.290
and continues to grow.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:50.960
For me, if you are not producing

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.330
audio, written words, videos and pictures

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:57.020
for those seven to eight
platforms today, on a daily basis,

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.040
consistently, you are
on the beginning process

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.940
of complete irrelevance in our society.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:06.760
And for me, not having a
complete religion around that

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.260
is a grave, grave mistake.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:11.650
The other thing that becomes
unbelievably interesting

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:16.330
for me this morning at this
keynote is the fact that

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:20.640
in this market, Helsinki,
Finland, and the Nordics,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.360
as a whole, the two platforms
that I obsess the most over

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.750
today for scale, Facebook
and Instagram, the ads,

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.380
the influencer marketing
market and the organic reach

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:37.430
are all dramatically more
interesting in this market

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:39.330
than they are in other parts of Europe,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.840
definitely than they are
in the U.S. and Canada

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.170
and other parts of South America.

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:47.140
If I could really get you
to a very simple place

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:50.790
this morning, the only thing
you actually, fundamentally

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.270
have to understand is that Facebook ads,

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.930
both in Instagram and
Facebook form, in this market,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:02.640
are so under-priced that
even if you are average

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.780
at the creative that you
put behind those ads,

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:09.030
you will get disproportionate
return on your business

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:10.603
whether you're B2B or B2C.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.520
To me, that is a moment
that I haven't seen

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:18.220
since early Google.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:21.090
When I think about my career,

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:25.460
when I launched an e-commerce
wine business in 1996,

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.330
I was able to build a
business that was doing

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:30.650
three million dollars a
year on 10% gross profit.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.060
So, $300,000, before expenses,

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:38.900
a business of that size
with a $14,000 a year

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:42.590
marketing budget in year
one, I was able to build

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.160
that business from a three to
a 60 million dollar business

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:50.160
in five years, 100% on the back
of email and Google AdWords.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:54.520
When I look back at that
story, I have enormous amounts

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.560
of regret because I genuinely
believe that should have been

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.770
three to 200 million dollars.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:03.130
The mistake I made in my
youth, the mistake I made

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:06.340
that many people made was
I just didn't understand

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:10.987
how good of a deal Google
AdWords were from 2000 to 2004.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:15.300
They were so remarkably under-priced

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.270
that I wasn't able to quantify,
especially since I had

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:22.810
no prior experience, that I
was buying beachfront property,

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:27.810
that I was digging up gold
at enormously low prices.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:33.040
That underlining
understanding, in hindsight,

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.120
is what drives me this
morning to suffocate

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.540
anybody's excuses in this
room to why Facebook,

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:43.490
to why Instagram doesn't work.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:45.010
Or for the people, actually, watch this,

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.670
how many people here have run Facebook ads

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.560
and it didn't, for-say,
work as well as you wanted?

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.700
Which, I'm sure is for
a lot, raise your hands.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.690
Actually, raise them high,
please, I want to see this.

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.000
So, I think this is
what's super important.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.320
There's an enormous
percentage of people here

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.930
who've already done what
I'm spitting as the thing

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:05.740
you need to be doing.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.240
But I want to make sure
that 20% of this room

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.520
that just raised their hand understood

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.240
that there's so many variables
that go into something

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:17.610
not being successfully, most
of all, the biggest variable is

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.820
you probably did not do a good
job executing the campaign.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:25.990
At the end of the day, when you
think about ROI of something

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:29.540
the ROI of anything is always
far greater for the people

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.260
that are good at it versus bad at it.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:36.780
The ROI of a piano for me is zero.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:41.780
The ROI of a piano for Elton
John is over a billion dollars.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:45.650
So, to me, the concern I
have is that we're sitting,

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:50.610
right now, in this prime era
where everybody in this room,

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:54.060
and I mean everybody,
government employees down to

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.520
entrepreneurs selling
a tie on the internet,

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:02.520
all have unbelievable
white space in this medium

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.380
and I believe that there's
a gross misunderstanding.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:09.740
And I challenge and predict

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.270
that everybody in this room
that doesn't run a hefty amount

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.450
of Facebook ads, Instagram ads,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.880
and Instagram influencer campaigns

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.530
during this next 24 to 36 months

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:25.460
will look back at this speech and this era

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.670
with the same regret that
I have through my body

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.200
to what I did from 2001 to 2004.

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.080
Which is, you may win and do quite well

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:36.260
but, please, don't misunderstand it,

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:40.240
you've left an enormous
opportunity on the table.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:43.970
It's so funny, in the U.S.,
I've been pontificating this

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.664
for about three or four
years and, by the way,

00:12:46.664 --> 00:12:48.970
just for those who are scoring at home,

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:52.770
the cost of the CPM on Facebook
has gone up substantially

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.840
in the last 24 to 36 months
globally and, definitely,

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:59.903
in the U.S., yet, still
grossly less than it deserves.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:04.740
It's interesting, I pontificate
this and big companies see,

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.832
wow, let's clap it up,
this is fucking clean.

00:13:07.832 --> 00:13:09.941
(audience applauds)

00:13:09.941 --> 00:13:12.770
(laughs)

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:14.320
- [Jan] Alright, thank you, guys.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:16.630
I'll be back a little bit later.

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:19.210
- In the U.S., I have the
biggest COs in the world

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:22.750
arguing to me that this platform
doesn't sell their product,

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.860
that Facebook's not ROI positive,

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:27.210
meanwhile, in the other
side of their mouth,

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.420
they're complaining to me
