I was traveling across the
galaxies to find the one.

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:30.563
Can you help me?

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:35.100
- Yes.
- Oh, that's me?

00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:36.347
- [Gary] Shit, sorry, yes, I can--

00:33:36.347 --> 00:33:38.290
- [Woman] R2-D2, bring me back.

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:40.533
- Oh, she's gone.
- I love it.

00:33:42.110 --> 00:33:44.333
- Alright, that was quick one.

00:33:45.320 --> 00:33:46.820
Do we have another question

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:50.633
and not Star Wars interrupters?

00:33:52.306 --> 00:33:54.960
(electricity buzzes)

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:55.980
- [Alexei] Oh, hi there.

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:58.250
My name is Alexei Habarki and I'm here

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:00.060
to ask you a question, Gary.

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:02.680
So, my question for you
is what is the next thing

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:04.660
that marketers will ruin?

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:05.820
- Huh.
- Thank you.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:07.308
- You're welcome.

00:34:07.308 --> 00:34:11.430
So, I often talk about marketers
ruin everything, right.

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:14.580
My career is trying to figure
out where you're spending

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:17.550
your time organically, and
then, I'm trying to figure out

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:20.130
how to penetrate it and sell shit.

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:23.210
And so, I think the thing that,

00:34:23.210 --> 00:34:25.970
you know, there's nothing that's
very black and white to me

00:34:25.970 --> 00:34:29.290
but if you look at email, the
reason we've all protected

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:32.730
our text messages so much
is we gave up our email

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.980
and marketers ruined it, right.

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:38.330
I think that the next thing
will clearly be voice.

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:41.510
But, I think, what we've
learned from Facebook is,

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:44.920
the great misunderstanding
of Facebook's era

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:48.310
is that the algorithm changes
because the only asset

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:51.610
Facebook is your attention
and as soon as they see that

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.600
they're putting too much ads
or things that you don't want,

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:57.320
they start eliminating
it to keep you there.

00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:00.330
I think that has changed
my historic point of view

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:02.090
on marketers ruin everything.

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:06.020
I think everybody here is
starting to intuitively understand

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:09.020
that through marketing, we
need to bring more value.

00:35:09.020 --> 00:35:11.960
It's less about interrupting
and being selfish

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:13.990
and it's more about bringing value.

00:35:13.990 --> 00:35:16.750
But to answer it directly,
I think the voice space

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:19.350
is going to be the big
battle ground where we'll get

00:35:19.350 --> 00:35:21.930
a lot of value and then,
marketers will come in

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.760
and try to figure out how to
get our attention and buy shit.

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:28.360
- It's like you're going to
ask Alexa or something, first,

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:30.740
going to give you an ad before
it gives you the answer.

00:35:30.740 --> 00:35:32.970
- I don't know if, I don't
think Amazon's going to give us

00:35:32.970 --> 00:35:36.050
an ad, I don't think when
Bruce says, hey, I want some

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:39.830
toilet paper or toothpaste
that Amazon's right move

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:43.630
is saying, Bruce, you
should try the new Crest.

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:44.640
Nobody's going to want that.

00:35:44.640 --> 00:35:47.340
I think where Amazon has
unbelievable leverage

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:51.000
is when he says, I want
three tubes of toothpaste,

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.670
they're gonna just send
whatever the fuck they want.

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:56.160
Because I think the thing
that you'll be stunned by

00:35:56.160 --> 00:35:59.440
is there's very few things
that you actually very much

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:02.113
care about to stay on the same brand.

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:04.560
And everybody's different.

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:08.330
Somebody in the audience here
may only use this one shampoo,

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:09.520
others won't.

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:10.560
I...

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:12.225
Is this the pair?

00:36:12.225 --> 00:36:15.120
Yeah, so, I can't believe
that I bought three pairs

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:18.400
of jeans on Amazon
without saying the brand.

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:22.860
I thought I cared about the
brands of jeans that I wore but,

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:25.700
in the moment, as my behavior's changing,

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:29.810
I was taught by myself
that it's not a category

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:32.270
where I care versus cereal.

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:35.600
I would never say to Google:
Google send me some cereal.

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:38.800
I'm going to say, send me
fucking Captain Crunch.

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:41.250
So, I think it's going
to be interesting to see

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:43.570
where it goes, but I don't
think it's going to be an ad,

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.100
I think it's going to be
the leverage of sending

00:36:45.100 --> 00:36:46.590
whatever you want.

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:49.240
The blue bar at the to of Google

00:36:49.240 --> 00:36:51.250
is not going to be suggestions anymore,

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.060
it's where you're going to go.

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:56.273
And, as you can image, that's
going to be very expensive.

00:36:57.610 --> 00:36:59.410
- [Jan] Alright, thank you.

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:01.223
Next question, please.

00:37:02.272 --> 00:37:04.880
